iency of new Bacillus thuringiensis var israelensis and Bacillus sphaericus formulations against Afrotropical anophelines in Western Kenya</article-title><source>Trop Med Int Health</source><year>2003</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>37</fpage><lpage>47</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12535249</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1365-3156.2003.00979.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>WHO</collab></person-group><article-title>International programme on chemical safety (IPCS): Microbial pest control agent Bacillus thuringiensis</article-title><source>Environmental Health Criteria</source><year>1999</year><volume>217</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>105</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mulla</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Thavara</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Tawatsin</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Chomposri</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Su</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Emergence of resistance and resistance management in field populations of tropical Culex quinquefasciatus to the microbial control agent Bacillus sphaericus</article-title><source>J Am Mosq Control Assoc</source><year>2003</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>39</fpage><lpage>46</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12674533</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mukabana</surname><given-names>WR</given-names></name><name><surname>Kannady</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Ijumba</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Mathenge</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Kiche</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Nkwengulila</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Mboera</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Mtasiwa</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamagata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>van Schayk</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Knols</surname><given-names>BG</given-names></name><name><surname>Lindsay</surname><given-names>SW</given-names></name><name><surname>Caldas de Castro</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mshinda</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Tanner</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Fillinger</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Killeen</surname><given-names>GF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecologists can enable communities to implement malaria vector control in Africa</article-title><source>Malar J</source><year>2006</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>9</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16457724</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1475-2875-5-9</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>NMCP</collab></person-group><source>National Malaria Control Strategic Plan 2002-2007</source><year>2002</year><publisher-name> Gambia Department of State for Health and Welfare</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hougard</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Mbentengam</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Lochouarn</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Escaffre</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Darriet</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Barbazan</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Quillevere</surname><given-names>D</given-name